WEBVTT

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:18.640
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.030
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.980
And takes me

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.950
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.070
To the home of wind

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.450
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.750
Matthew Cuthbert had intended to
welcome his adopted boy at the station.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.000
But there was a red-haired,
skinny girl there instead.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.100
How beautiful!

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.640
I didn't think I could see such a wonderful lake.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.010
Why is the carriage trembling so much?

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.500
I really like this feeling.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.260
Please stop here for a while?

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.650
That pear tree is like a girl clothed in white,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.730
viewing her reflection upon the lake.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.700
It'll be late if we don't set off soon.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:27.290
Let's go.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.750
Goodnight, Mr. lake of shining waters.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.930
I say good night to my favourite things,

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.130
because I feel it would make them happy.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.020
Look! That lake is like smiling in gratitude.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.270
That's Barry's pond.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.900
But I feel "Lake of Shining Waters"
is more suitable.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.430
Yes, that is the right name for it. I know
because of the thrill.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.300
When I hit on a name that suits
exactly it gives me a thrill.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.350
Do things ever give you a thrill?

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.310
Well now, yes.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.590
It always kind of gives me a thrill
to see them ugly white grubs

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.300
that spade up in the cucumber beds.
I hate the look of them.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:40.240
Oh, I don't think that can be exactly
the same kind of a thrill.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.530
But why do other people call it
Barry's pond?

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.550
Well now, I reckon because Mr. Barry
lives up there in that house.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.000
Green Gables is right behind it.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.100
Has Mr. Barry any little girls?

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.010
Well, not so very little either...
About my size.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:08.640
He's got one about eleven.
Her name is Diana.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.220
Oh! What a perfectly lovely name!
Like the moon goddess!

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.800
Well now, I dunno. There's something
dreadful heathenish about it.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.000
We're pretty near home now.
That's Green Gables over...

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.800
Please, don't tell me!

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.500
Let me guess.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.380
I'm sure I'll guess right.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.500
That's it, isn't it?

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.800
Well now, you've guessed it!

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.880
But I reckon Mrs. Spencer described
it so's you could tell?

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.900
No, she didn't...

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.800
All she said might just as well have
been about most of those other places.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.200
I hadn't any real idea what it
looked like.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.300
But just as soon as I saw
it I felt it was home.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.900
Oh, it seems as if I must be in a dream.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.700
Do you know, my arm must be black
and blue from the elbow up,

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.980
for I've pinched myself so
many times today.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.300
Very little while a horrible sickening
feeling would come over me

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.110
and I'd be so afraid it was all a dream.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.960
But it is real and we're nearly home.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.600
Listen to the trees talking in their sleep.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.300
What nice dreams they must have!

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:12.200
Matthew Cuthbert, who's that?
Where is the boy?

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:18.800
There wasn't any boy. There was only her.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.970
No boy! But there must have been a boy!

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.000
We sent word to Mrs. Spencer to bring a boy.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.900
Well, she didn't. She brought her.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.400
I asked the station-master.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.000
What did you say?
You didn't ask her the reason?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.800
Well...I was late...

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.800
And I had to bring her home.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.660
Well, what are we going to do?

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.040
You don't want me!

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.900
You don't want me because I'm not a boy!

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.090
I might have expected it.
Nobody ever did want me.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:11.280
I might have
known it was all too beautiful to last.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:18.020
I  might have known
nobody really did want me.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.930
Oh, what shall I do?
I'm going to burst into tears!

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.280
Burst into tears she did.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.660
Marilla and Matthew looked at each
other deprecatingly across the stove.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:03.110
Neither of them knew what to say or do.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:07.490
Finally Marilla stepped lamely into the breach.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.200
Well, well, there's no need to cry so about it.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.040
Yes, there is need!

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.840
You would cry, too!

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:26.520
If you were an orphan and had come to
a place you thought was going to be home..

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.990
and found that they didn't want you
because you weren't a boy

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:30.820
you would cry too.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:36.760
Oh, this is the most tragical thing
that ever happened to me!

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:44.060
Well, don't cry any more. We're not going to
turn you out-of-doors tonight.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:48.800
What is her name?

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.620
W-well. I have yet to ask her.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.370
What's your name?

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:06.610
Will you please call me Cordelia?

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.320
Call you Cordelia? Is that your name?

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:15.470
No-o-o, it's not exactly my name...

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.940
but I would love to be called Cordelia.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.010
It's such a perfectly elegant name.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:25.210
I don't know what on earth you mean.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.310
If Cordelia isn't your name, what is?

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.790
Anne Shirley.

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:37.980
But, oh, please do call me Cordelia.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.280
It can't matter much to you what you call me

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.790
if I'm only going to be here a little while, can it?

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:49.950
And Anne is such an unromantic name.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.000
Unromantic fiddlesticks!

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:57.620
Anne is a real good plain sensible name.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:00.350
You've no need to be ashamed of it.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.540
Oh, I'm not ashamed of it,

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.770
only I like Cordelia better.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:09.720
I always imagine that my name is Cordelia.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:13.330
But when I was young I
used to imagine it was Geraldine.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.760
But if you call me Anne...

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.640
please call me Anne spelled with an E.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.270
What difference
does it make how it's spelled?

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.630
Oh, it makes such a difference.

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:29.630
It looks so much nicer.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:33.180
When you hear a name pronounced
can't you always see it in

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.960
your mind, just as if it was printed out?

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.560
I can.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.390
If you'll only call me Anne spelled with an E,

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:44.880
I shall try to reconcile myself to
not being called Cordelia.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.210
Very well, then, Anne spelled with an E,

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:51.970
can you tell us how this
mistake came to be made?

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.600
Were there no boys at the asylum?

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:56.580
Oh, yes, there was an abundance of them.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:13:02.000
But Mrs. Spencer said distinctly that you
wanted a girl about eleven years old.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:10.540
You don't know how delighted I was.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.500
I couldn't sleep all last night for joy.

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:22.200
Why didn't you tell me at the station that
you didn't want me and leave me there?

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.000
If I hadn't seen the White Way of Delight

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.470
and the Lake of Shining Waters it
wouldn't be so hard.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:34.200
What on earth does she mean?

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:37.850
She...she's just referring to...

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:43.110
some conversation we had on the road.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:49.000
I'm going out to put the mare in, Marilla.
Have tea ready when I come back.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:56.410
Did Mrs. Spencer bring anybody
over besides you?

00:13:56.410 --> 00:14:00.130
She brought Lily Jones for herself.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:05.090
Lily is only five years old and she is
very beautiful and had nut-brown hair.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:11.750
If I was very beautiful and
had nut-brown hair would you keep me?

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:17.960
No. We want a boy to help
Matthew on the farm.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:21.320
A girl would be of no use to us.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.800
Take off your hat. I'll lay it and
your bag on the hall table.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.380
You're not eating anything!

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:32.810
I can't.

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:37.800
I'm in the depths of despair.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:42.030
Can you eat when you are in the
depths of despair?

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.720
I've never been in the depths of despair,
so I can't say.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.630
Weren't you?

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:53.960
Well, did you ever try to imagine
you were in the depths of despair?

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:55.550
No, I didn't.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:02.520
Then I don't think
you can understand what it's like.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.790
It's a very uncomfortable feeling indeed.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:08.750
When you try to eat,
a lump comes right up in your throat

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.780
and you can't swallow anything.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.390
Not even if it was a chocolate caramel.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.250
I had one chocolate caramel once two years ago,

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.600
and it was simply delicious.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:30.180
I've often dreamed since then that I
had a lot of chocolate caramels,

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.050
but I always wake up just
when I'm going to eat them.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.620
I do hope you won't be
offended because I can't eat.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:46.910
Everything is
extremely nice, but still I cannot eat.

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:59.440
I guess she's tired.

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.500
Best put her to bed, Marilla.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:05.200
Yes. Let's do that.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:39.800
I suppose you have a nightgown?

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.400
Yes, I have two.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:48.600
But they're fearfully skimpy. There is
never enough to go around in an asylum...

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.000
I hate skimpy night-dresses.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:59.300
But one can dream just as well
in them as in lovely trailing ones

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:02.600
with frills around the neck.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.500
That's one consolation.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:09.880
Well, undress as quick as you can and go to bed.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.960
I'll come back in a
few minutes for the candle.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.700
I daren't trust you to put it out yourself.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.600
You'd likely set the place on fire.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:48.000
Good night.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.160
How can you call it a good night?

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.800
You know it must be the very
worst night I've ever had!

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.170
Well, this is a pretty kettle of fish...

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:30.770
This is what comes of sending
word instead of going ourselves.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:37.790
One of us will have to drive over and
see Mrs. Spencer tomorrow, that's certain.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:41.430
This girl will have
to be sent back to the asylum.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.460
Yes, I suppose so.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:48.300
You suppose so!

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.410
Don't you know it?

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:56.590
Well now, she's a real nice little thing, Marilla.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:04.810
It's kind of a pity to send her back
when she's so set on staying here.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:10.750
Matthew Cuthbert, you don't mean to
say you think we ought to keep her!

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:17.110
Well, now, no, I suppose not--not exactly...

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:19.400
I suppose...

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:28.410
we could hardly be expected to
keep her.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:32.120
I should say not.
What good would she be to us?

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:37.080
We might be some good to her.

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:38.810
Matthew Cuthbert...

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:42.510
I believe that child has bewitched you!

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:49.010
Well now, she's a real interesting little thing.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:54.580
You should have heard her talk
coming from the station.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.810
Oh, she can talk fast enough.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:59.650
I saw that at once.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:03.570
It's nothing in her favour, either.

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:07.380
I don't like children who have so much to say.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:09.120
And I don't want an orphan girl.

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:13.940
No, she's got to be despatched straight-way
back to where she came from.

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:19.580
I could hire a French boy to help me,

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:24.190
and she'd be company for you.

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:30.410
I'm not suffering for company,
and I'm not going to keep her.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:35.080
Well now, it's just as you say.

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:42.690
I'm going to bed.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:22.600
Gazing at the scene of the cherry bloom
and dandelion outside the window,

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:24.650
Anne finally regained her happy heart.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.490
Next of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:29.760
"Morning at Green Gables"

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:30.740
Please watch!
